    "['peer [parameter1] reflect-client', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols bgp group <group-name> neighbor 172.16.2.1 cluster <cluster-id>', 'parent_command': ['set protocols bgp group [parameter1]', 'family [parameter2] [parameter3]'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols bgp group <group-name> neighbor 172.16.2.1 cluster <cluster-id>\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Juniper, BGP route reflection is configured per neighbor under a BGP group.\\n2. You'll need to:\\n   - Replace `<group-name>` with your BGP group name\\n   - Replace `<cluster-id>` with your route reflector cluster ID (typically the router-id of the reflector)\\n3. The equivalent Juniper command achieves the same function of configuring 172.16.2.1 as a route reflector client, but the syntax is different from Huawei's direct `reflect-client` command.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols bgp group <group-name> neighbor 172.16.2.1 cluster <cluster-id>'}]]": 1,
    "['peer [parameter1] capability-advertise add-path send', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols bgp group <group-name> neighbor 172.16.2.1 add-path send', 'parent_command': ['set protocols bgp group [parameter1]', 'family [parameter2] [parameter3]'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols bgp group <group-name> neighbor 172.16.2.1 add-path send\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you would typically need to specify a BGP group name (replace `<group-name>` with your actual group name). The command enables BGP ADD-PATH capability for sending multiple paths to the neighbor 172.16.2.1, which is the functional equivalent of Huawei's `capability-advertise add-path send` command.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols bgp group <group-name> neighbor 172.16.2.1 add-path send'}]]": 1,
