    "['peer [parameter1] reflect-client', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols bgp group internal-peers neighbor 172.16.4.2 cluster 5.5.5.5', 'parent_command': ['set protocols bgp group [parameter1]', 'family [parameter2] [parameter3]'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols bgp group internal-peers neighbor 172.16.4.2 cluster 5.5.5.5\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, BGP route reflection is configured by setting a `cluster` ID (typically the router-ID) on the neighbor, which makes it a route reflector client.\\n- The command assumes the BGP neighbor is already defined in a group called \"internal-peers\" (adjust group name as needed for your specific configuration).\\n- The cluster ID is set to the router-ID (5.5.5.5) from the original Huawei configuration.\\n- This is the Juniper equivalent of making 172.16.4.2 a route reflector client.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols bgp group internal-peers neighbor 172.16.4.2 cluster 5.5.5.5'}]]": 1,
    "['peer [parameter1] reflect-client', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols bgp group internal-peers neighbor 172.16.5.2 cluster 5.5.5.5', 'parent_command': ['set protocols bgp group [parameter1]', 'family [parameter2] [parameter3]'], 'root': 'set protocols bgp group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols bgp group internal-peers neighbor 172.16.5.2 cluster 5.5.5.5\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nIn Juniper, the equivalent of Huawei\\'s `reflect-client` is achieved by:\\n1. Adding the neighbor to a BGP group (here named \"internal-peers\")\\n2. Setting the cluster ID (which is typically the router-id of the route reflector, 5.5.5.5 in this case)\\n\\nThe command effectively marks 172.16.5.2 as a route reflector client, just like in the Huawei configuration.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols bgp group internal-peers neighbor 172.16.5.2 cluster 5.5.5.5'}]]": 1,
